# Lorekeeper Page Formats

> Generated by `lore schema` — locale: ko
> Regenerate after changing `vault.locale`: `lore schema`, which REPLACES this file wholesale — nothing added here survives it, and `lore validate` prompts for it on every run. Vault-specific instructions belong in a file this tool does not generate.

**This vault is written in Korean.** Every word added to a page goes in that language — a summary, a theme, a concept's synthesis, an exploration — whatever language the source arrived in. Two things are not translated by it. Source content is quoted as it stands, and a NAME is not prose: a concept's name is the form its own source writes, so a term a source writes in another language keeps its spelling here (§ Concept convergence says what follows from that).

Pipeline-managed semantic sections carry `llm_inputs`: `<key>` identifies the queued input and `<key>_done` identifies the input whose answer was published. Equality signals completion; a non-empty body does not. For every queued kind, `/lore-process` stages a typed result and `lore queue apply` validates and publishes the body and completion marker together. Never edit a queued target or stamp its markers directly. `lore doctor` reports unanswered inputs, including pending work. Directly authored pages omit this block until a machine writer establishes it.

A page's `llm_inputs` map is machine-coordination state: each key records the input a section is owed against and its `_done` companion the input a section was written from. Never author or edit those values. The applier owns completion and generation-policy provenance; `lore queue regenerate --task-id ID` requests regeneration from retained input when its guards still match. A concept page's `aliases` is renderer-written and always present, carrying the title and every other name the page answers to; on a page you author yourself, write `aliases` only where the page really does answer to another name.

Each table's `Owner` column names who fills that section's body: `machine` = `lore` writes it, under whichever command produces the page (`lore graph backlinks-sync` for concept; `lore ingest` for daily, document, work-log; `lore synthesis` for weekly-review, monthly-review, quarterly-review, annual-review); `LLM` = an agent generates its content; queued results are published by `lore queue apply`. A page you author DIRECTLY has no pipeline behind it, so you fill EVERY section yourself, `machine` ones included — except a concept's `## 출처` section and its `source_count`, which `lore graph backlinks-sync` re-derives wholesale from the forward links on citing pages every time it runs, so leave those empty and let it.

A concept's `## 핵심` is yours to write when you create the page and is NOT yours after that. It is owed against the set of pages citing the concept, so once that set moves, `lore graph backlinks-sync` queues a rewrite and a drain writes the section from the sources themselves. Write what the page's own material establishes; do not write a synthesis you would not want restated from the evidence.

Every page's `type` frontmatter is its page-format id — exactly the `## {type}` names below (`concept`, `daily`, `document`, …). It is the one REQUIRED key of the Open Knowledge Format, so any OKF consumer can classify the vault's pages without Lorekeeper-specific knowledge.

## Links

Every internal reference is an inline markdown link `[Display](relative/path.md)` whose destination is RELATIVE TO THE CONTAINING PAGE'S DIRECTORY and always carries the `.md` extension — the one form Obsidian, GitHub, and OKF consumers all resolve. Never write `[[wikilinks]]`. A concept link from a page is `[{Name}]({concepts-dir}/{slug}.md)`, where `{concepts-dir}` is the relative path to `wiki/concepts` from that page and `{slug}` is the slug of the concept name (rule below). Destinations with spaces or parens are percent-encoded (`%20`, `%28`, `%29`); non-ASCII slugs stay verbatim.

## Navigating this vault

Ask for what you need and drill in from the answer — navigate, don't scan every file:
- `lore wiki search <query> --json` — the pages a query reaches, best match first. `matched` says WHY each is here: `identity`/`name` is a page the query names, `summary` one that opens by stating it, `text` one that merely mentions it. Start here for a named topic. Output is bounded by `--limit`; live calls scan their scope; `--snapshot` reuses a prepared lexical index. Query meaningful terms separately when a full question has no results.
- `lore wiki browse --json` — browse by title with category/type counts. Both browse and search accept `--category`, `--type`, `--source-status`, `--limit` and `--offset`. Facet counts describe the query before metadata filters; `total` counts filtered matches and `next_offset` locates the next page. Counts include empty metadata under an empty key. Live pagination reflects current files; `--snapshot` paginates captured revisions.
- `lore wiki search <literal> --body --json` — exact raw Markdown body lines, including code and link destinations. Case-sensitive by default; select --regex or --ignore-case explicitly. --context 0..5 bounds surrounding lines; each result supplies physical line/column, clipping flags and file_revision. Totals count matching lines/pages, not semantic claims. This live scan rejects --snapshot and reads the full selected scope even with a small --limit.
- `lore wiki prepare` — capture a reusable SQLite lexical search snapshot. Pass its `snapshot` to search/browse with `--snapshot`. It contains captured revisions, not live changes; prepare again after edits or before a new investigation. Missing or mismatched snapshots fail explicitly.
- `lore wiki read <page.md>` — paginated CommonMark outline (20 entries by default). Select `--section ID` for up to 4000 Unicode characters including nested sections. Follow `next_offset` with `--offset`; pass the returned `file_revision` as `--revision` on every subsequent read. Search hits also carry this revision. A mismatch requires fresh retrieval, not reuse of the old section id. Source provenance is returned alongside the content.
- `lore graph --json neighbors <vault-relative.md>` — direct incoming/outgoing links across managed pages, including daily evidence. Narrow with `--type concept` or `--direction incoming|outgoing|mutual` before paginating with `--limit` and `--offset`. Types match declared metadata exactly; an empty type selects undeclared pages. `formats` counts all neighbors before filters. Incoming/outgoing include mutual links; a link's direction does not establish its semantic meaning.
- `lore wiki links <source.md> --target <target.md>` — inspect explicit link occurrences as JSON with source line numbers, literal context and file_revision. Omit --target to see all internal links. Supports --limit and --offset; pass --revision from the previous response to reject pagination across source edits. For incoming neighbors inspect the neighbor as source. A link does not establish support or contradiction; read its context.
- `wiki/map.md` — concepts grouped by citation cluster (the graph's emergent structure), with at most 100 entries per page and details under `catalog/map/`. Cluster membership is navigation, not a semantic relationship assertion.
- `wiki/index.md` — entry to a complete catalog grouped by category. Each generated page contains at most 100 entries; larger vaults use a navigation tree under `catalog/`. Follow the displayed ranges. Generated catalog pages are not evidence or graph nodes.
- `wiki/log.md` — reverse-chronological timeline of when each knowledge node entered the vault. Each page contains at most 100 entries; follow `catalog/log/` links for detail. Child addresses are regenerated navigation positions; cite original knowledge pages.
From an entry point, open the pages it links and follow their links. Regenerate every entry point with `lore wiki refresh`.

